WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.530
This is ContextLoop:

2
00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:07.219
a working demonstration of why your AI agent is only as good as the data behind it.

3
00:00:07.639 --> 00:00:10.518
In the next few minutes: the problem, the solution,

4
00:00:10.518 --> 00:00:13.510
and how each layer of the architecture fits together.

5
00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:19.745
Meet Northstar Industrial, a mid-market maker of valves, bearings and pumps,

6
00:00:19.745 --> 00:00:22.520
with about a hundred and twenty business customers.

7
00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:26.727
Its data lives in four systems that don't agree with each other:

8
00:00:26.727 --> 00:00:31.225
SAP for orders and deliveries, Salesforce for accounts, Zendesk for support,

9
00:00:31.225 --> 00:00:35.131
and a Postgres carrier service that holds the real delivery times.

10
00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:40.171
Now the chief operating officer asks the company's AI agent one question.

11
00:00:40.591 --> 00:00:44.325
Which of our strategic accounts are at risk because of late shipments,

12
00:00:44.325 --> 00:00:46.832
and what should the account teams do this week?

13
00:00:47.752 --> 00:00:52.769
Answering it correctly needs all four systems, one agreed definition of on time,

14
00:00:52.769 --> 00:00:57.034
one agreed definition of strategic, and data fresh enough to act on.

15
00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:03.319
Give the agent raw SQL access, and it plans sixteen queries on its own,

16
00:01:03.319 --> 00:01:07.581
working out from scratch what the data means. It answers fluently,

17
00:01:07.581 --> 00:01:10.164
with invented precision. And it's wrong.

18
00:01:10.584 --> 00:01:12.859
It counts cancelled deliveries as late.

19
00:01:12.859 --> 00:01:17.000
It uses the planned ship date instead of the carrier's actual delivery.

20
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.441
It adds euros to dollars, counts a duplicate account twice,

21
00:01:20.441 --> 00:01:23.416
and never notices the support data is two days old.

22
00:01:23.836 --> 00:01:27.564
Across six golden questions, it averages 2.7 out of 6,

23
00:01:27.564 --> 00:01:30.256
and nothing in the answer tells you so.

24
00:01:30.676 --> 00:01:32.580
A better prompt doesn't fix this.

25
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.388
The model can't know what on time or strategic means at Northstar.

26
00:01:38.088 --> 00:01:41.688
The fix is a trusted data foundation, built in four layers.

27
00:01:42.108 --> 00:01:46.601
Move: Fivetran lands every source reliably and incrementally,

28
00:01:46.601 --> 00:01:48.811
with deletes and history kept.

29
00:01:49.231 --> 00:01:55.096
Transform and trust: dbt models, tests and a semantic layer define each number once.

30
00:01:55.516 --> 00:02:00.425
Context: MCP servers give the agent those definitions, the lineage,

31
00:02:00.425 --> 00:02:02.330
and how fresh the data is.

32
00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:08.102
And activate: the answer flows back into the tools the account teams already use.

33
00:02:08.522 --> 00:02:11.522
Same model, same question. With the foundation in place,

34
00:02:11.522 --> 00:02:15.434
the agent needs five calls instead of sixteen, and scores six out of six.

35
00:02:17.534 --> 00:02:21.966
Before we walk the diagram, here is what each technology does,

36
00:02:21.966 --> 00:02:25.827
and how this demo uses it. First, transform and trust.

37
00:02:26.247 --> 00:02:27.789
DuckDB is the warehouse:

38
00:02:27.789 --> 00:02:32.738
one embedded database file holding the raw landed tables and every dbt model.

39
00:02:32.738 --> 00:02:35.887
It's where the naive agent went wrong in act one.

40
00:02:36.307 --> 00:02:40.924
dbt Core builds the trusted data in layers. Staging cleans each raw table,

41
00:02:40.924 --> 00:02:44.231
intermediate models apply Northstar's business rules,

42
00:02:44.231 --> 00:02:48.787
and marts are the ready-to-use tables: twenty-seven tested models in all.

43
00:02:48.787 --> 00:02:52.531
Each trap is fixed in one named model and guarded by a test.

44
00:02:52.951 --> 00:02:58.981
On top sits the semantic layer, MetricFlow. It defines eight metrics once, in YAML,

45
00:02:58.981 --> 00:03:05.084
and compiles the SQL for every query, so no one, human or agent, rewrites the logic.

46
00:03:05.504 --> 00:03:10.676
After every build, the trusted marts are published as Apache Iceberg tables. Trino,

47
00:03:10.676 --> 00:03:15.349
a second engine, reads the same tables and returns the same rows as DuckDB.

48
00:03:16.169 --> 00:03:19.307
Context. MCP, the Model Context Protocol,

49
00:03:19.307 --> 00:03:25.046
is an open standard for handing an agent tools. Three MCP servers run here,

50
00:03:25.046 --> 00:03:26.730
in private containers.

51
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.062
The official dbt MCP server explains models and lineage.

52
00:03:31.062 --> 00:03:35.742
The metrics server serves governed numbers, compiled by MetricFlow.

53
00:03:35.742 --> 00:03:41.260
And the freshness server reads the sync log and reports how old each source is.

54
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.975
Claude, the same model as in act one, reaches them through a small bridge,

55
00:03:45.975 --> 00:03:47.890
and never touches the raw tables.

56
00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:53.287
Activate. A diff-based reverse ETL sync reads the account risk mart,

57
00:03:53.287 --> 00:03:57.796
previews the changes, and writes only what changed into Salesforce,

58
00:03:57.796 --> 00:04:02.103
with an audit log, then posts a summary to Slack. At a customer,

59
00:04:02.103 --> 00:04:04.459
Fivetran Activations does this job.

60
00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:11.029
Here is the architecture, left to right: four sources, then move,

61
00:04:11.029 --> 00:04:15.602
transform and trust, context, and activate, with the app underneath.

62
00:04:15.602 --> 00:04:19.098
Each act adds one layer, until the loop is complete.

63
00:04:20.118 --> 00:04:26.105
Act one, the confident wrong answer. The agent reaches straight into the raw tables,

64
00:04:26.105 --> 00:04:31.807
shown by the dashed line, with no definitions and no idea how fresh the data is.

65
00:04:32.527 --> 00:04:33.468
Act two, move.

66
00:04:33.468 --> 00:04:38.641
Fivetran Connector SDK connectors read SAP and the Postgres carrier database,

67
00:04:38.641 --> 00:04:41.731
with incremental syncs and checkpointed state.

68
00:04:42.151 --> 00:04:46.195
Every source lands the same way, with Fivetran's system columns.

69
00:04:46.195 --> 00:04:50.682
Deleted rows are flagged, not lost, and account tier keeps its history.

70
00:04:50.682 --> 00:04:54.032
A sync log records freshness and monthly active rows.

71
00:04:54.752 --> 00:04:57.508
Act three, transform and trust. Staging,

72
00:04:57.508 --> 00:05:02.884
intermediate and mart models turn the raw tables into tested, contracted data,

73
00:05:02.884 --> 00:05:05.985
and every trap is handled in one named place.

74
00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:11.550
The semantic layer defines on-time delivery rate, net revenue and account risk once.

75
00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:14.674
The trusted tables are published as Apache Iceberg,

76
00:05:14.674 --> 00:05:17.614
and Trino reads exactly the same rows as DuckDB.

77
00:05:18.334 --> 00:05:23.655
Act four, context. The same Claude agent now works through three MCP servers:

78
00:05:23.655 --> 00:05:29.252
the dbt MCP server for models and lineage, a metrics server for governed queries,

79
00:05:29.252 --> 00:05:31.878
and a freshness server for sync times.

80
00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:37.648
So it uses Northstar's definitions, and says when the data is too old to trust.

81
00:05:37.648 --> 00:05:40.019
Here is that request, step by step.

82
00:05:40.439 --> 00:05:42.671
The COO's question arrives from the app.

83
00:05:43.091 --> 00:05:46.250
The agent lists its tools from the three MCP servers.

84
00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:51.639
It reads the metric definitions, including exactly what strategic means.

85
00:05:52.059 --> 00:05:55.515
It checks freshness, and learns that Zendesk is two days behind.

86
00:05:55.935 --> 00:06:01.528
It queries the governed metric. MetricFlow compiles the SQL over the tested models.

87
00:06:01.948 --> 00:06:06.072
It fetches the lineage of on-time delivery rate from dbt.

88
00:06:06.492 --> 00:06:12.036
It writes the answer: accounts, drivers, next steps, the definitions it used,

89
00:06:12.036 --> 00:06:13.765
and a freshness warning.

90
00:06:14.185 --> 00:06:18.810
And the app scores it against the hidden truth: exactly the nine at-risk accounts.

91
00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:20.910
Act five, activate.

92
00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:26.632
A diff-based reverse ETL sync writes the at-risk list and next steps into Salesforce,

93
00:06:26.632 --> 00:06:31.142
and posts a summary to Slack, where the account teams already work.

94
00:06:31.862 --> 00:06:35.722
All of this runs on one laptop, in Docker. At a customer,

95
00:06:35.722 --> 00:06:38.296
every box has a production equivalent:

96
00:06:38.296 --> 00:06:43.985
Fivetran's native SAP connectors or hybrid deployment, the customer's own warehouse,

97
00:06:43.985 --> 00:06:47.168
the dbt platform and its hosted Semantic Layer,

98
00:06:47.168 --> 00:06:51.706
Iceberg on the Managed Data Lake Service, and Fivetran Activations.

99
00:06:54.006 --> 00:06:56.478
Reliable movement, governed transformation,

100
00:06:56.478 --> 00:06:58.893
and shared definitions the agent can read.

101
00:06:58.893 --> 00:07:02.746
That's what turns a confident wrong answer into one you can act on.

102
00:07:03.166 --> 00:07:06.983
The next step is a scoped pilot against your own success criteria,

103
00:07:06.983 --> 00:07:09.238
with named owners. This is ContextLoop.
